          <Pad Id="OvpoWHzetoGLk8ennuAu4r" Bounds="74,130,712,58" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="https://doc.stride3d.net/latest/en/api/Stride.BepuPhysics.BepuSimulation.html#Stride_BepuPhysics_BepuSimulation_Overlap__1___0__Stride_BepuPhysics_Definitions_RigidPose__System_Span_Stride_BepuPhysics_CollidableStack__Stride_BepuPhysics_CollisionMask_">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Link</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
          <Pad Id="FZtbP03kzOAP6HYGHgrMWO" Bounds="782,895,539,652" ShowValueBox="true" isIOBox="true" Value="Overlap places a shape at a fixed pose and asks &quot;what is intersecting this volume right now&quot;. Same shape pins as the sweeps, but instead of Origin/Direction/Max Distance there's just a Position.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;It outputs a Spread&lt;OverlapInfo&gt;, and each entry carries more than a hit test: the Penetration Direction and Penetration Length ,the minimal push that would separate the query shape from that collidable (a minimum translation vector, accessible via Split (OverlapInfo)). A compound collidable can appear several times, once per overlapping child shape; the extra Collidables output is the plain &quot;who's in there&quot; list when you don't care about the details.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Typical uses:&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Area effects: a sphere overlap at an explosion point lists everything in the blast radius in one call,  then feed the collidables into ApplyImpulse, scaled by penetration depth for a cheap falloff.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Placement / spawn checking: before teleporting or spawning a crate, overlap its box at the target pose. Empty spread = the space is free. Even better, the penetration data tells you how to fix it: shifting the target by Penetration Direction × Penetration Length is exactly the nudge that makes it fit.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;Proximity sensing without physics bodies: enemies within reach, pickups near the player, anything inside a security zone, a per-frame overlap needs no extra collidable, no contact handler, no entity in the scene.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;One-frame melee hits: on the attack frame, overlap a box in front of the character; everything in the spread took the hit. No trigger setup, no waiting for a physics step.&#xD;&#xA;&#xD;&#xA;That last point is the practical difference to the Trigger node: Trigger is persistent and event-based , a real collidable in the simulation reporting entered/exited transitions on physics steps. Overlap is immediate and stateless from the patch's view, an on-demand question about an arbitrary volume, polled whenever you ask, with no notion of enter or exit. Zone gameplay usually wants Trigger; instantaneous queries and placement logic want Overlap.">
            <p:TypeAnnotation LastCategoryFullName="Primitive" LastDependency="VL.CoreLib.vl">
              <Choice Kind="TypeFlag" Name="String" />
            </p:TypeAnnotation>
            <p:ValueBoxSettings>
              <p:fontsize p:Type="Int32">9</p:fontsize>
              <p:stringtype p:Assembly="VL.Core" p:Type="VL.Core.StringType">Comment</p:stringtype>
            </p:ValueBoxSettings>
          </Pad>
